   "line": [
    "Rfd1"
   ],
   "spoken": "The key vocabulary: a type-one piece is defended by nothing, a type-two by a single piece — never a pawn — and the queen counts as undefended always, because it must flee any attacker. Harp on those loose pieces and the tactics find themselves.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "Scan for type-one and type-two pieces every move; they are where combinations live."
  },
  {
   "ply": 46,
   "t": 310.5,
   "fen": "r5k1/pp2qp2/2b3p1/3R3p/5Q1P/2P2P2/P3r1P1/3R3K w - - 2 24",
   "line": [
    "Bc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "So the bishop goes to c6, not b5 — c6 is defended by a pawn, and a piece is only truly safe when a pawn guards it.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "A piece is genuinely secure only when a pawn, not another piece, defends it."
  },
